The false-postcondition mutation built and failed for the right reason: the residual is exactly `0.0 #Equals floatMod(N, 1.0)`, so the result is not vacuous. A separate body mutation (`return 0.0`) also rebuilt cleanly and failed the original result obligation, while an automated normalization check confirmed the candidate macro exactly matches submitted `solution.mpy`. One optional attempt to make Haskell evaluate concrete floating arithmetic hit its documented missing `FLOAT.div` hook; I’m treating that only as evidence that `floatMod` remains a trust boundary, not as a candidate failure—the required symbolic proof and LLVM concrete checks both completed.
Stages 1–6 are complete. The required proof closed fresh; program/AST pinning, exhaustive inventory, body sensitivity, and false-result non-vacuity checks are recorded. I am consolidating the trust ledger and final verdict.
